WEBVTT
TITLE Algebraic Boolean Minimisation — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The GATE 2027 syllabus names three ways to minimise a Boolean function: the algebraic technique, the Karnaugh map, and the tabular method.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
All three start from the same object — a function given by a truth table or a canonical expression — and all three aim at a shorter expression.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Only two of them tell you when to stop. The algebraic route stops when nothing looks applicable, which is not the same thing as minimal.

4
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.000
A literal is one occurrence of a variable, negated or not. ab′c′ has three literals; xy + x′z has four.

5
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Cost is counted in literals because a literal is a gate input. Terms count gates; literals count the inputs those gates need.

6
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The canonical form of n variables costs n literals per minterm, so a five-minterm three-variable function starts at fifteen literals.

7
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Eight identities, each an equality, each usable as a rewrite: replace the left side by the right side anywhere inside the expression.

8
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Five of them reduce: combine, absorb, eliminate a literal, remove a consensus term, and De Morgan to cross between the two normal forms.

9
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Because every one is an equality, no rewrite can change the function. The page tests 250 applications on three-variable pairs and finds none that moves a truth table.

10
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Start with a′b + a′b′c′d′ + abcd′: ten literals in three terms, none of them canonical.

11
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Factor a′ out of the first two terms: a′(b + b′c′d′) + abcd′. Nothing has been deleted yet — the shape changed.

12
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Now eliminate: b + b′c′d′ = b + c′d′, because b is a single literal. Then factor again and eliminate again.

13
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Multiplying out gives a′b + bcd′ + a′c′d′: eight literals, and the exhaustive search confirms eight is the minimum.

14
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Absorption: X + XY = X. If X is 1 both terms are 1; if X is 0 both terms are 0, so the second term never decides anything.

15
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Eliminating a literal: X + X′Y = X + Y. If X is 1 both sides are 1; if X is 0 then X′ is 1 and both sides are Y.

16
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:18.000
But X must be one literal. With a longer X you must factor first: a′b′ + a′bc′ = a′(b′ + bc′) = a′(b′ + c′).

17
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:26.000
The consensus of xy and x′z is yz: keep every literal the two terms share, and drop the variable they disagree on.

18
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Row by row: the consensus term is 1 only on rows where one of the two original terms is already 1.

19
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:42.000
That is the whole proof: yz is 1 only when y and z are both 1, and then x is either 1 or 0, so xy or x′z is 1.

20
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:50.000
So the term can go: six literals become four, and the model confirms the two expressions agree on all eight rows.

21
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.000
The consensus exists only when the two terms disagree on exactly one variable. xy and x′y′ disagree on two.

22
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Apply the rule blindly and the empty term 1 appears: xy + x′y′ becomes xy + x′y′ + 1, which is the constant 1.

23
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:14.000
At x = 1, y = 0 the function is 0 and the constant is 1: minterm 5. Count the oppositions before you remove anything.

24
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Five terms, ten literals: a′b′ + ac + a′c′ + b′c + ab. The minimum is six. Two routes both reach a correct answer.

25
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Route one removes consensus terms first: a′c′ + ab + b′c, six literals. That is the minimum.

26
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Route two absorbs first and then finds nothing applicable: a′b′ + ac + a′c′ + ab, eight literals, still equal to F.

27
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.000
wx + xy + x′z′ + wy′z′: nine literals, and not one reducing rule applies. No combine, no absorb, no elimination, no consensus term.

28
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.000
The escape is to add a term: wz′ is the consensus of wx and x′z′, so adding it is safe. Eleven literals — worse.

29
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Now wy′z′ is absorbed by wz′: wx + xy + x′z′ + wz′, eight literals.

30
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.000
And wz′ is itself the consensus of wx and x′z′, so it goes too: wx + x′z′ + xy, six literals, the minimum.

31
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Every non-constant three-variable function was tried: 255 of them, from the canonical form, applying the four reducing rules until none applies.

32
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
148 of the 255 end above the minimum: 58 per cent. Adding the best single consensus term fixes 108 of those, and leaves 40 still above.

33
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
One witness is kept: the function that is 1 everywhere except minterm 001. The algebraic route stops at nine literals; the answer is a + b + c′, three.

34
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:44.000
One function: Σ(0,1,2,5,6,7) on a, b, c. Canonical form is six terms of three literals — eighteen.

35
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
The algebra reaches six literals by removing consensus terms. The map and the table reach the same six, and can prove it is the smallest.

36
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
And the minimum is not always unique: 28 of the 255 three-variable functions have more than one six-literal answer, or more than one answer at their minimum.

37
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Factoring is a rewrite too: AB + AC + AD is six literals in four gates; A(B + C + D) is four literals in two.

38
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:16.000
But depth is a cost as well: AB + AC + D is five literals at depth two, while A(B + C) + D is four literals at depth three.

39
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:24.000
And a redundant term is sometimes kept on purpose: for xy + x′z, the consensus yz holds the output at 1 while x changes, so a glitch cannot pass.

40
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Five ordered steps: count the literals, combine, absorb, eliminate, remove a consensus term — and repeat from the top.

41
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
If nothing applies and you are still long, factor, or add a consensus term and try again. That is a decision, not a rule.

42
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Then check the answer on one assignment where it should be 0 and one where it should be 1. Two expressions that disagree on one row are different functions.

